/* deterministic camera math - MaidenPool, batch 2.
   Contract: identical bits on every conforming GL 4.3 driver.
   Only pinned operations (+, -, *, fma on precise values), integer
   and bit arithmetic, exact selections (abs/min/max/floor/clamp),
   and constants delivered as bit patterns. Newton seeds come from
   bit tricks, never from the driver's loose reciprocal - a
   deterministic wrong guess refined by exact arithmetic is a
   deterministic answer.
   Outside the contract: NaN payloads (flushed downstream), 0/0,
   atan(0,0), log of a non-positive. Each returns SOMETHING
   deterministic on the cards measured, but the camera must not
   lean on it.
   AND A DOMAIN, which this list used to omit: det_sin/det_cos/
   det_tan are defined for |x| < 2^15*pi/2 = 51471. Beyond that
   the argument reduction stops being exact, and the list said
   nothing while the functions returned 3147, then 4e30, then Inf.
   The limit was 2^22*pi/2 = 6588397 until 2026-08-24, when the
   reduction was rebuilt on truncated splits to remove its need for
   a fused multiply-add; those splits are exact only while |k| is
   under 2^15, and that is now the binding constraint rather than
   the shift trick. No plate in the atlas passes an argument above
   about 804, measured by lowering this guard and re-rendering.
   They now return (0, 1) there - deterministic, bounded, and
   obviously not a measurement. Nothing this camera renders comes
   near the limit; the point is that a contract naming no domain is
   a contract that cannot be checked.
   Denormals ARE inside the contract, and getting them there took
   work: every rescale that used to multiply a denormal by 2^48 now
   builds the scaled value from its bits, because GLSL permits a
   driver to flush denormals and two drivers disagreeing is the one
   failure this library exists to prevent. */

/* THE DOMAIN, WHICH USED TO BE UNSTATED AND UNENFORCED.
   The shift trick rounds to a nearest INTEGER only while t stays in
   the binade [2^23, 2^24), where the spacing is 1.0. RND_MAGIC is
   1.5*2^23, so that holds exactly while |x*(2/pi)| < 2^22, i.e.
   |x| < 2^22*pi/2 = 6588397.3. Above it, t moves into binades of
   spacing 2, 4, 8..., k quantizes to that spacing, and the reduced
   |r| leaves the pi/4 domain the minimax was fitted on. Measured
   with the exact emulator: |x| in [2^26,2^27) gives |det_sin| =
   3147, det_sin(1e12) = -4.19e30, det_sin(1e20) = -Inf. A sine
   returning 4e30 is worse than any driver builtin, which at least
   stays bounded (finding 69).

   Worse for this library's actual promise: `int(k)` is UNDEFINED in
   GLSL once |k| >= 2^31 (|x| >= 3.37e9), and q selects the quadrant
   - so two stacks disagreeing about an out-of-range conversion
   return DIFFERENT SIGNS from det_sin. That is a parity break, not
   an accuracy one, and parity is the whole product (finding 70).

   So the domain is enforced rather than described. Outside it the
   answer is the deterministic, bounded, obviously-not-a-measurement
   pair (0, 1) - and the header now lists the limit, because a
   contract that omits its own domain is how this went unnoticed.
   `!(abs(x) < LIM)` rather than `>=` so a NaN argument takes this
   branch too; NaN compares false against everything. */
void det_sincos(float x, out float s_out, out float c_out){
  if (!(abs(x) < @SINCOS_LIM)) { s_out = 0.0; c_out = 1.0; return; }
  precise float t = fma(x, @TWO_OVER_PI, @RND_MAGIC);
  precise float k = t - @RND_MAGIC;
  int q = int(k) & 3;
  /* FOUR STAGES ON TRUNCATED SPLITS, so no product needs rounding.
     This was three stages on FULL-PRECISION pieces of pi/2, where
     k*PIO2_1 wants ~47 bits and only a single-rounded fma can carry
     the residual the next stage cancels. That made the whole camera
     depend on a guarantee five of eleven measured stacks do not
     honour, and on those the reduction lost the residual outright -
     det_sin wrong by up to 2.9e9 ULP past |x| > 2pi.
     Each piece here has its low 15 bits zeroed, so it carries 9
     significant bits and k*P is EXACTLY a float32 for |k| < 2^15.
     Nothing is shed, so nothing needs capturing, and plain * and -
     suffice - which every stack computes identically. */
  precise float r = fma(-k, @PIO2_1, x);
  r = fma(-k, @PIO2_2, r);
  r = fma(-k, @PIO2_3, r);
  r = fma(-k, @PIO2_4, r);
  precise float r2 = r * r;
  precise float ps = fma(r2, @SS3, @SS2);
  ps = fma(r2, ps, @SS1);
  precise float r3 = r2 * r;
  precise float ss = fma(r3, ps, r);
  precise float pc = fma(r2, @CC3, @CC2);
  pc = fma(r2, pc, @CC1);
  precise float w  = fma(r2, @NHALF, 1.0);
  precise float r4 = r2 * r2;
  precise float cc = fma(r4, pc, w);
  s_out = (q == 0) ? ss : (q == 1) ? cc : (q == 2) ? -ss : -cc;
  c_out = (q == 0) ? cc : (q == 1) ? -ss : (q == 2) ? -cc : ss;
}
float det_sin(float x){ float s, c; det_sincos(x, s, c); return s; }
float det_cos(float x){ float s, c; det_sincos(x, s, c); return c; }

/* A DENORMAL SCALED BY 2^48, WITHOUT ARITHMETIC ON A DENORMAL.
   GLSL's range-and-precision rules let a driver flush any
   denormalized value input into a shader, or generated by any
   operation in one, straight to zero. That is implementation-
   defined - precisely the freedom this library exists to revoke -
   and three sites leaned on `x * 2^48` surviving it: det_sqrt's
   prescale, det_log2's rescue, and now det_recip. On a flushing
   driver det_sqrt returned 0 where a non-flushing one returned
   sqrt(x): a parity break rather than a value error (finding 73).

   The scaling is exact in the integers and needs no denormal to be
   an operand. A denormal's bits ARE its mantissa, and its value is
   ux * 2^-149, so ux * 2^48 = ux * 2^-101; the normal float that
   carries it is built from the leading bit position. findMSB is an
   integer operation with a fully specified result, which is the
   kind of thing this library is allowed to stand on. */
float det_scale48(uint ux){
  uint lz   = uint(findMSB(ux));                 /* 0..22 */
  uint mant = (ux << (23u - lz)) & 0x007FFFFFu;
  return uintBitsToFloat(((lz + 26u) << 23u) | mant);
}

/* reciprocal: bit-trick seed, four exact Newton rounds */
float det_recip(float b){
  uint ub = floatBitsToUint(b);
  uint sg = ub & 0x80000000u;
  uint m  = ub & 0x7FFFFFFFu;
  if (m >= 0x7F800000u)            /* inf or nan -> signed zero */
    return uintBitsToFloat(sg);
  if (m == 0u)                     /* zero -> inf */
    return uintBitsToFloat(0x7F800000u | sg);
  /* A DENORMAL DIVISOR IS NOT AN INFINITE RECIPROCAL, and treating
     it as one was wrong for three quarters of them. 1/b is finite
     and representable for every |b| >= 1/FLT_MAX = 2.9387e-39, which
     is 6,291,455 of the 8,388,607 positive denormals. This returned
     Inf for all of them: det_div(3e-39, 1.5e-39) gave Inf where the
     answer is 2.0, and through det_atan - where mx can be denormal -
     det_atan(9.99994610111476e-41, 2.000003233207595e-40) returned
     -Inf where the answer is 0.4636448 (finding 71).
     Scaled up by an exact power of two so the seed sees a normal
     number, and the answer scaled back the same way. Where 1/b truly
     does overflow, r * 2^48 is Inf, which is the right answer. */
  precise float pre = 1.0;
  float ax;
  if (m < 0x00800000u) {
    ax = det_scale48(m);
    m = floatBitsToUint(ax);
    pre = @TWOP48;
  } else {
    ax = uintBitsToFloat(m);
  }
  /* THE SEED SUBTRACTION IS UNSIGNED, AND WITHOUT THIS IT WRAPS.
     0x7EF127EA - m for m > 0x7EF127EA (|b| > 1.603e38, an ordinary
     finite normal) goes modulo 2^32 and the seed becomes garbage:
     NaN over one band, and over another a large NEGATIVE normal, so
     Newton diverges and det_recip(3.4028235e38) returned -Inf - the
     WRONG SIGN, over 0.44% of all finite float32. It degrades before
     it wraps, too: above 2^126 the seed itself is denormal and
     Newton, which at best doubles its correct bits per round, cannot
     climb out.
     det_sqrt's analogous seed cannot wrap (0x5F375A86 - (mm>>1u), and
     mm>>1u <= 0x3FBFFFFF), and det_sqrt and det_log2 both carry a
     rescale for out-of-seed-range inputs. This is the one that was
     left unguarded.
     So: fold the argument down by 2^32 before seeding and fold the
     answer back afterwards. Both factors are exact powers of two, so
     the only cost is at the very bottom, where 1/b is denormal
     anyway and the product lands there or flushes - deterministically,
     which is the property this library sells. */
  precise float post = 1.0;
  if (m >= 0x7E000000u) {                    /* |b| >= 2^125 */
    ax = ax * uintBitsToFloat(0x2F800000u);  /* x 2^-32, exact */
    m  = floatBitsToUint(ax);
    post = uintBitsToFloat(0x2F800000u);
  }
  precise float r = uintBitsToFloat(0x7EF127EAu - m);
  precise float e;
  e = fma(-ax, r, 1.0); r = fma(r, e, r);
  e = fma(-ax, r, 1.0); r = fma(r, e, r);
  e = fma(-ax, r, 1.0); r = fma(r, e, r);
  e = fma(-ax, r, 1.0); r = fma(r, e, r);
  precise float rp = r * post * pre;
  return uintBitsToFloat(floatBitsToUint(rp) ^ sg);
}

float det_div(float a, float b){
  uint mb = floatBitsToUint(b) & 0x7FFFFFFFu;
  precise float r = det_recip(b);
  precise float q = a * r;
  /* AND ONLY REFINE A FINITE ESTIMATE. When a/b overflows on two
     finite operands, q is already +-Inf; the correction term is then
     fma(-b, Inf, a) = -+Inf and the refinement folds that back as
     Inf*r + Inf, which is NaN. An overflow is not a NaN, and the
     difference is not cosmetic here: ADDF passes the result through
     clamp(), GLSL min/max on a NaN is UNSPECIFIED, and a saturated
     pixel that is deterministic on one driver and not on another is
     a parity failure rather than a value error.
     Scoped deliberately to the finite-operand case. det_div(inf, 2.0)
     is NaN before this line - det_recip returns a signed zero for an
     infinity, so q = inf * 0 is already NaN - and that is a separate
     question about what a reciprocal of infinity should be. */
  if (mb - 0x00800000u < 0x7F000000u && !isinf(q) && !isnan(q)) {
    q = fma(fma(-b, q, a), r, q);
  }
  return q;
}

/* sqrt: rsqrt bit seed, four Newton rounds, one Heron step.
   Denormals prescaled by 2^48 so the seed sees a normal number. */
float det_sqrt(float x){
  uint ux = floatBitsToUint(x);
  if (ux == 0u || ux == 0x80000000u) return uintBitsToFloat(ux);
  if (ux == 0x7F800000u) return x;             /* +inf */
  if (ux > 0x7F800000u || (ux & 0x80000000u) != 0u)
    return uintBitsToFloat(0x7FC00000u);       /* nan / negative */
  precise float xs = x;
  float post = 1.0;
  /* det_scale48, not `x * 2^48`: the multiply needs the denormal
     operand to survive, which GLSL lets a driver refuse. On a
     flushing driver xs was 0, mm 0, and every Newton round
     degenerated - det_sqrt returned 0 where a non-flushing driver
     returned sqrt(x) (finding 73). */
  if (ux < 0x00800000u) { xs = det_scale48(ux); post = @TWOM24; }
  uint mm = floatBitsToUint(xs);
  precise float r = uintBitsToFloat(0x5F375A86u - (mm >> 1u));
  precise float hx = 0.5 * xs;
  precise float t;
  t = hx * r; t = fma(-t, r, 1.5); r = r * t;
  t = hx * r; t = fma(-t, r, 1.5); r = r * t;
  t = hx * r; t = fma(-t, r, 1.5); r = r * t;
  t = hx * r; t = fma(-t, r, 1.5); r = r * t;
  precise float s = xs * r;
  s = fma(fma(-s, s, xs), 0.5 * r, s);
  return s * post;
}

/* exp2: exact split, cephes polynomial, exponent by bits */
float det_exp2(float x){
  precise float xc = clamp(x, -150.0, 129.0);
  precise float t = xc + @RND_MAGIC;
  precise float k = t - @RND_MAGIC;
  precise float f = xc - k;
  int e = int(k);
  precise float p = @E2P0;
  p = fma(f, p, @E2P1);
  p = fma(f, p, @E2P2);
  p = fma(f, p, @E2P3);
  p = fma(f, p, @E2P4);
  p = fma(f, p, @E2P5);
  precise float y = fma(f, p, 1.0);
  if (e > 128) return uintBitsToFloat(0x7F800000u);
  /* e == 128 IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY OVERFLOW. The split rounds to the
     NEAREST integer, so f runs [-0.5, 0.5] and y runs about
     [0.707, 1.414]: for arg in [127.5, 128) the exponent lands on 128
     while y < 1, and the product is finite - 2^127.535 is 2.46e38,
     comfortably inside float32. Returning inf there was wrong for
     nine of the probe's inputs and invisible in the published ULP
     figure, which masked |x| > 16.
     2^128 has no float32 to name it, so the scale is applied in two
     steps. The doubling still overflows to inf when it genuinely
     should, which is the case y >= 1. */
  if (e == 128) {
    /* BOTH steps carry `precise`, and the second one is the whole
       point. Qualifying only `h` left `h * 2.0` free to be folded
       back into y * (2^127 * 2.0) - and 2^127 * 2.0 is inf, so the
       constant fold reintroduced exactly the overflow this branch
       exists to avoid. Measured on NVIDIA: the emulator returned
       2.465e38 and the card returned inf from the same source. */
    precise float h = y * uintBitsToFloat(uint(127 + 127) << 23u);
    precise float r = h * 2.0;
    return r;
  }
  if (e >= -126) return y * uintBitsToFloat(uint(e + 127) << 23u);
  if (e < -150) return 0.0;
  y = y * @TWOM126;
  return y * uintBitsToFloat(uint(e + 253) << 23u);
}

/* log2: exponent peeled by bits, mantissa folded to
   [sqrt2/2, sqrt2), atanh series through the exact divide */
float det_log2_ef(float x, out float f){
  uint ux = floatBitsToUint(x);
  if (ux == 0u) { f = 0.0; return uintBitsToFloat(0xFF800000u); }  /* -inf */
  if (ux >= 0x7F800000u || (ux & 0x80000000u) != 0u) {
    f = 0.0;
    return uintBitsToFloat((ux == 0x7F800000u) ? 0x7F800000u
                                               : 0x7FC00000u); }
  float e0 = 0.0;
  if (ux < 0x00800000u) {                    /* denormal rescue */
    /* in the integers, so a driver that flushes denormals cannot
       turn this into log2(0) = -inf while another returns the
       answer (finding 73) */
    ux = floatBitsToUint(det_scale48(ux)); e0 = -48.0;
  }
  float e = float(int(ux >> 23u) - 127) + e0;
  precise float m = uintBitsToFloat((ux & 0x007FFFFFu)
                                    | 0x3F800000u);
  if (m > @SQRT2) { m = m * 0.5; e = e + 1.0; }
  precise float num = m - 1.0;
  precise float den = m + 1.0;
  precise float s = det_div(num, den);
  precise float z = s * s;
  precise float p = fma(z, @NINTH, @SEVENTH);
  p = fma(z, p, @FIFTH);
  p = fma(z, p, @THIRD);
  precise float q = fma(z, p, 1.0);
  precise float twos = s + s;
  precise float ln = twos * q;
  f = ln * @LOG2E;
  return e;
}
/* det_log2 KEEPS ITS ORIGINAL BODY, DUPLICATED ON PURPOSE.
   The obvious move is `float f; e = det_log2_ef(x, f); return e + f;`
   and it is WRONG. The shipped tail is `((ln) * (@LOG2E) + (e))` as
   one unqualified return expression, which a driver may contract into
   a single-rounded fma; recombining a pre-rounded f gives two
   roundings and a different answer on 0.53% of inputs, by 1 ULP.

   Measured 2026-08-25, and the 1 ULP is not the point - the blast
   radius is. Swapping only det_pow moved starfield's worst bin by 15
   deposits; also swapping det_log2 moved it by 16,660,144, about 15%
   of that bin. A one-ULP shift in log2 relocates an enormous
   accumulated value in a chaotic plate, and det_log2 is EQUALLY
   accurate both ways (p99 1.0, max 3). That is churn for nothing:
   44 plates that never call det_pow would move for no gain.

   So the series is written twice. The duplication is the cheap half
   of this trade. NOTE the shipped tail's contractability is a real
   latent hazard - it is an unqualified expression deciding a value -
   but all 23 columns agree on it today, so changing it is unforced
   risk and belongs in its own deliberate experiment. */
float det_log2(float x){
  uint ux = floatBitsToUint(x);
  if (ux == 0u) return uintBitsToFloat(0xFF800000u);     /* -inf */
  if (ux >= 0x7F800000u || (ux & 0x80000000u) != 0u)
    return uintBitsToFloat((ux == 0x7F800000u) ? 0x7F800000u
                                               : 0x7FC00000u);
  float e0 = 0.0;
  if (ux < 0x00800000u) {
    ux = floatBitsToUint(det_scale48(ux)); e0 = -48.0;
  }
  float e = float(int(ux >> 23u) - 127) + e0;
  precise float m = uintBitsToFloat((ux & 0x007FFFFFu)
                                    | 0x3F800000u);
  if (m > @SQRT2) { m = m * 0.5; e = e + 1.0; }
  precise float num = m - 1.0;
  precise float den = m + 1.0;
  precise float s = det_div(num, den);
  precise float z = s * s;
  precise float p = fma(z, @NINTH, @SEVENTH);
  p = fma(z, p, @FIFTH);
  p = fma(z, p, @THIRD);
  precise float q = fma(z, p, 1.0);
  precise float twos = s + s;
  precise float ln = twos * q;
  /* ASSIGNED, NOT RETURNED. In a return expression this fma - after
     the bake unfuses it to a mul and an add - reaches no precise
     destination, and a front end that does not inline (ANGLE,
     measured 2026-08-26) may legally re-fuse it. Three of five
     Vulkan backends did, and nine log-heavy plates split into a
     second self-consistent answer. The precise local closes the
     license; on inlining stacks the bits are identical either way. */
  precise float r = fma(ln, @LOG2E, e);
  return r;
}

/* atan2: octant fold through the exact divide, odd minimax,
   exact reconstructions */
float det_atan(float y, float x){
  precise float ay = abs(y);
  precise float ax = abs(x);
  precise float mn = min(ay, ax);
  precise float mx = max(ay, ax);
  if (mx == 0.0) return 0.0;
  precise float r = det_div(mn, mx);
  precise float z = r * r;
  /* atan(r) = r + r*z*q(z), NOT r * p(z).
     The old form multiplied r by a polynomial whose constant term was
     0.99997726, so for small r - where atan(r) tends to r - the answer
     came back scaled by that constant. A fixed 382 ULP that never
     improved as r shrank, worst exactly where the function is
     simplest. Splitting off the leading r makes small arguments right
     by construction and leaves q to carry only the correction. */
  precise float q = fma(z, @ATQ7, @ATQ6);
  q = fma(z, q, @ATQ5);
  q = fma(z, q, @ATQ4);
  q = fma(z, q, @ATQ3);
  q = fma(z, q, @ATQ2);
  q = fma(z, q, @ATQ1);
  q = fma(z, q, @ATQ0);
  precise float rz = r * z;
  precise float a = fma(rz, q, r);
  if (ay > ax) a = @PIO2_F - a;
  if (x < 0.0) a = @PI_F - a;
  /* THE SIGN BIT, NOT THE COMPARISON. `-0.0 < 0.0` is false, so a
     negatively-signed zero used to skip the negation: det_atan(-0.0,
     -1.0) returned +pi where atan2 gives -pi, and det_atan(-0.0, 1.0)
     returned +0.0 where atan2 gives -0.0 (finding 105). A full 2*pi
     jump, and sitting in the worst possible place - one ULP away the
     negative denormal is handled correctly (det_atan(-1.4e-45, -1.0)
     = -pi), so the angle flipped exactly AT the value a negative
     quantity underflows to. Plates that colour by angle - domain,
     invjulia, penrose all call pal(atan(z.y, z.x)/TAU + 0.5, ...) -
     put a single sample at the opposite end of the palette, in the
     same place on every card, which is the kind of speckle that reads
     as a dead pixel rather than as a bug.
     atan(0,0) stays out of contract per the header; atan2(-0, x) for
     x != 0 never was. */
  return ((floatBitsToUint(y) & 0x80000000u) != 0u) ? -a : a;
}

/* EXACT PRODUCT AS A HI+LO PAIR, WITHOUT fma.
   Dekker 1971. fma was never required - it is the shortcut. Split
   both operands so every partial product fits in 24 bits, and the
   error term falls out of exact arithmetic.

   THE SPLIT IS A BITMASK, NOT Veltkamp's multiply. Veltkamp computes
   hi = c - (c - a) with c = a*(2^12+1), which is algebraically `a`;
   a backend permitted to reassociate may fold the whole thing away
   and silently destroy the split. Masking is an integer operation
   that no float reassociation rule can touch. It is also cheaper,
   and it cannot overflow the way c = 4097*a can above 2^115.

   ONLY VALID BECAUSE p = 24 IS EVEN. Masking gives each half 12
   bits and 12+12 = 24 fits. At odd precision one half needs
   ceil(p/2) and hi*hi needs p+1 bits: measured 15% of products wrong
   at p=13. NEVER port this to a float64 library (p=53) - use
   Veltkamp there. That is exactly why the literature standardised on
   Veltkamp, which is precision-agnostic.

   Every step is its own `precise` local so no pair of them can be
   contracted into an fma; a fused step here is MORE accurate and
   therefore WRONG, because the error term would no longer be the
   exact residual. */
float det_split12(float a){
  return uintBitsToFloat(floatBitsToUint(a) & 0xFFFFF000u); }
float det_twoprod(float a, float b, out float lo){
  precise float p  = a * b;
  precise float ah = det_split12(a);
  precise float al = a - ah;
  precise float bh = det_split12(b);
  precise float bl = b - bh;
  precise float t1 = ah * bh;
  precise float t2 = t1 - p;
  precise float t3 = ah * bl;
  precise float t4 = t2 + t3;
  precise float t5 = al * bh;
  precise float t6 = t4 + t5;
  precise float t7 = al * bl;
  precise float e  = t6 + t7;
  lo = e;
  return p; }

/* composites - deterministic by composition */
float det_tan(float x){ float s, c; det_sincos(x, s, c);
                        return det_div(s, c); }

/* pow, with the product carried to double width.
   MEASURED 2026-08-25, before: p50 6 ULP, p99 52, max 100 over
   |y*log2(x)| spanning [0, 64+); the "1 ULP p99" this library
   advertised holds only inside |y*log2(x)| <= 1, which is what the
   old harness happened to sample.

   The mechanism is amplification, not a bad log2: an absolute error
   in the exponent becomes a RELATIVE error in the result, scaled by
   |y*log2(x)|*ln2 - up to ~89. Collapsing y*log2(x) into one float32
   therefore throws away exactly the bits that matter. Carrying the
   product as h+l and folding l back through 2^l = 1 + l*ln2 (exact
   to second order, since |l| <= ulp(h)/2) recovers them.

   det_log2's own error is untouched by this and remains the floor on
   how good pow can get. */
float det_pow(float x, float y){
  /* EDGE CASES TAKE THE OLD PATH, unchanged. det_log2_ef returns
     +-inf or nan in `e` for x <= 0, inf or nan, and det_twoprod on an
     infinity produces inf - inf = nan in its residual, which would
     turn pow(0, 2) into nan where it used to be 0. Short-circuit
     before any of the double-width machinery sees a non-finite. */
  uint px = floatBitsToUint(x);
  if (px == 0u || px >= 0x7F800000u || (px & 0x80000000u) != 0u)
    return det_exp2(y * det_log2(x));

  float f;
  precise float e = det_log2_ef(x, f);
  /* y*log2(x) = y*e + y*f, computed WITHOUT ever forming log2(x) as
     one float. That sum is where the precision went: `e` is an exact
     integer up to +-175 and `f` is under 0.5, so collapsing them
     absorbs f's low bits into e's exponent - and the exponent is
     exactly what gets amplified by ln2*|y*log2(x)|, up to ~89x.
     Splitting the product in two keeps both parts at full width. */
  float l1, l2;
  precise float h1 = det_twoprod(y, e, l1);
  precise float h2 = det_twoprod(y, f, l2);
  /* twoSum of h1 and h2: exact, branch-free, no fma */
  precise float s  = h1 + h2;
  precise float bb = s - h1;
  precise float u1 = s - bb;
  precise float u2 = h1 - u1;
  precise float u3 = h2 - bb;
  precise float er = u2 + u3;
  precise float lo = (l1 + l2) + er;
  precise float E  = det_exp2(s);
  precise float c  = lo * @LN2;
  /* E * (1 + c), NOT E*c + E - see det_exp, same hazard. When the
     result is small the correction term E*c lands in the denormals
     and a driver is permitted to flush it, discarding the whole
     correction. Forming (1 + c) first keeps the arithmetic near 1
     where nothing underflows. */
  precise float m  = 1.0 + c;
  /* pinned into a precise local before returning - see det_exp;
     an unqualified return expression is reassociated back to
     E + E*c and the correction underflows away. */
  precise float res = E * m;
  return res; }
/* AND THE COMPOSITES CARRY `precise` TOO, which they did not until
   2026-08-22. Written as one-liners, `fma(-x, x, 1.0)` here and
   `fma(-y, q, x)` in det_mod had NO qualifier on their results - and an
   unqualified fma is single-rounded on NVIDIA and iris and is NOT on
   radeonsi and llvmpipe. That is the one guarantee this whole library
   is built on, left off two of its own functions.
   Measured: det_mod on radeonsi differed from the qualified form on
   27,871 of 65,536 inputs. det_acos agreed in a small probe and
   disagreed inside `hyper`, which is the context dependence contraction
   has everywhere else - so it is a latent hazard rather than a
   sometimes-correct function.
   THE FIX IS HASH-PRESERVING ON NVIDIA AND IRIS: both already
   single-round an unqualified fma, so every det_ hash on those stacks
   is unchanged, det_sin still 27c0f355 and det_cos still a71fe904.
   What changes is that radeonsi now agrees with them. */
/* acos via (1-x)(1+x), NOT 1 - x*x.
   MEASURED 2026-08-25: p50 80 ULP, p99 451, max 511 for x in
   [0.999, 1]. Two things go wrong at once there and they multiply.
   `1 - x*x` cancels - x*x rounds first, losing the low bits before
   the subtraction, and at x = 0.99999994 the true 1-x^2 is 1.2e-7
   against a rounding error of 6e-8, so half the answer is noise. And
   acos(x) tends to 0 as x tends to 1, so that error is measured
   against a vanishing result.

   (1-x)(1+x) is the same quantity with neither problem: 1-x is EXACT
   by Sterbenz for x in [0.5, 2], 1+x is exact to a rounding, and the
   single product rounds once. Note this is better than a true fma
   here, not merely a replacement for one - fma(-x, x, 1.0) is
   correctly rounded but still measures a tiny difference of large
   numbers, while the factored form never forms the large numbers. */
float det_acos(float x){
  precise float u = 1.0 - x;
  precise float v = 1.0 + x;
  precise float t = u * v;
  precise float r = det_sqrt(t);
  return det_atan(r, x); }
float det_mod(float x, float y){
  precise float q = floor(det_div(x, y));
  precise float r = fma(-y, q, x);
  return r; }
